# Portfolio v6 — Architecture

> The log of decisions, bugs already paid for and how each experiment was
> tuned is in `history.mdc`. Read it before changing an experiment, the
> compositor or the studio.

The portfolio is a gallery of **composed artworks**. Experiments are the
materials; the studio layers them into a piece; a saved piece is featured on
Home with a short about and contact links.

## Stack
- Vite 8 + React 19 + TypeScript + Tailwind v4, **TanStack Router** with
  file-based routes in `src/routes/` (`src/routeTree.gen.ts` is generated by
  the Vite plugin — never edit it; it's excluded from Prettier and ESLint).
- three.js is the WebGL layer for the compositor and the poster/glass
  experiments; `@react-three/fiber` is used only by the poster experiment.
- `tsconfig` has `erasableSyntaxOnly`: no constructor parameter properties,
  no enums.

## Workflow this is built for
1. "Reproduce this idea" → a new **experiment** under `src/experiments/<slug>/`.
2. "New artwork with experiments 1 2 3" → layers in the **studio** (`/studio`).
3. Jin resizes, reorders, blends, masks and tunes in the studio.
4. "Adjust this layer just for this piece" → an **artwork-only layer variant**.
5. Save → `src/works/<slug>/artwork.json` + `thumb.jpg`, featured via the
   artwork's `featured` flag.

## Experiments (`src/experiments/`, `/experiments/$slug`)
- Each is registered in `registry.ts` and lazy-loaded.
- Tuning lives in a control store (`src/lab/controls.ts`): typed schema,
  values in localStorage, tilde-key panel (`ControlPanel`). "Save defaults"
  (dev only) writes `src/lab/presets/<store-id>.json`, which becomes that
  store's defaults.
- **An experiment that can be a layer keeps its drawing in a renderer class**
  separate from its page: `render(ctx, W, H, dtSeconds, values)` draws one
  frame in the units the caller already scaled to, and applies its own
  speed/pause. The page is a thin full-screen driver. Renderers must take
  their values as arguments — **never read a store's `.values` globally**,
  or a saved artwork would change when the experiment is retuned
  (`TerminalWindows` takes its terminal values through its constructor for
  this reason). `glassRenderer` still reads `terminalControls.values`; fix it
  before glass becomes a layer.

## Studio and compositor (`src/studio/`)
- `document.ts` — the artwork format. Frames are in **design units**
  (`DESIGN` = 800×1000, 4:5), not pixels, so a piece is identical at any
  display size; renderers draw in those units. Layer docs carry frame
  (`x y w h rot`), `opacity`, `blend`, `mask` and **`fills`** (see Fills below).
- `layer.ts` — the layer contract: a `LayerType` (schema, `initial()`,
  `create()`, `version`) whose instance renders into a `THREE.Texture`.
  `canvasLayer()` hosts any 2D renderer on an offscreen canvas sized to the
  frame. Bump a type's `version` when saved values would render differently;
  the compositor warns on a mismatch.
- `compositor.ts` — **one WebGL context, one canvas per artwork**. Background
  fill → each visible layer stacks its fills into an artwork-sized buffer
  (`STACK_FRAG`, one pass per fill) → `LAYER_FRAG` lays that buffer onto the
  artwork bottom to top (ping-pong render targets): shape cut, mask (another
  layer's finished stack, alpha or luminance, optionally inverted; a hidden
  layer still stacks if it's a mask source), blend mode → post (grain,
  vignette).
  Colour stays in sRGB values throughout (no colour management), so blend
  modes behave like a design tool's.
- Saved layer values are checked with `fit()` against the schema and filled
  from **schema defaults**, never from an experiment's live store. Save writes
  every value out in full.
- `registry.ts` — built-in layer types (grid, blueprint, terminal, video,
  solid/gradient) plus each artwork's **local variants**:
  `src/works/<slug>/layers/<name>.ts` default-exports a layer type and is
  addressed from that artwork only as `local/<name>`. A custom tweak for one
  piece goes there — copy the code it changes rather than editing a built-in,
  so no other artwork moves.
- **A layer is a shape with lists**, like Paper and Figma. Types are in `document.ts`;
 `fillsOf`, `filtersOf`, `strokesOf` and `shadowsOf` read every list, including the old fields
 on layers saved before the lists existed.
 - `fills`: `FillDoc`s, pixel-making layer types (solid/gradient or any experiment) with
 `opacity`, `blend` and `fillSpace` (`'canvas'` lays a fill across the whole artwork).
 - `filters`: `FilterDoc`s (blur, pixelate, mono, invert, posterize, threshold, dither;
 `FILTERS` in `studioSchemas.ts`) with an `input`:
 - `'layer'` changes the layer's fills before the cut, so the edge stays crisp.
 - `'background'` changes the artwork behind the layer, shown inside the shape. A shape
 with background blur is a lens.
 - `strokes`: `StrokeDoc`s, bands along the edge (`width`, `align`, `offset`, `fibers`),
 each **painted with its own fill stack**. Old paper core and stroke became an inside stroke
 with fibres, then the stroke.
 - `shadows`: `ShadowDoc`s (offset, blur, colour, opacity), up to 8.
 - `edge` now only changes the outline: tear and feather.
 - **Old layers:** `upgrade()` converts a document when the studio opens it, and Save writes
 lists. Migration must not call `Math.random`: it shifted other layers' seeded random draws
 and made a render diff look like a regression.

- **Render pipeline per layer** (`compositor.ts`), all in artwork-sized buffers:
 1. `DIST_FRAG`: the outline (shape, booleans, tear) as a distance at every pixel, in a
 HalfFloat buffer. Every later step that needs the edge reads this.
 2. `STACK_FRAG` steps: fills laid over → filters (blur is `BLUR_FRAG`, two directions) →
 cut to the outline → each stroke's paint stacked in its own buffer, then laid in its band.
 3. `LAYER_FRAG`: shadows (the distance buffer read at an offset), then the layer, masked and
 blended over the artwork.

 `field-notes` was checked against a pre-change render: no visible difference, only
 antialiasing along the torn edge.
- **Canvas size per artwork.** `Artwork.size` is in design units; when absent it's `DESIGN`
 (800×1000), which older work keeps. Read it with `sizeOf(doc)`, never `DESIGN` directly.
 - `ASPECTS` holds presets with the long side at 1000. The Artwork panel's Canvas section has
 them plus W/H.
 - `resizeArtwork` keeps layers where they are and stretches only layers that filled the old
 canvas.
- **Pan and zoom** (`ArtworkPlayer navigable`):
 - The canvas always covers the container, and the compositor renders just the visible
 rectangle, `setView(view, surround)`. Every pass maps a pixel to an artwork position with
 `artAt(uv, uView)` and reads buffers with `atArt(t, p, uView)`. The post pass paints the
 surround outside the artwork.
 - Cost stays the same at any zoom. Layer textures are drawn at pixels per design unit, so
 they sharpen as you zoom in, up to each one's size cap.
 - Input: pinch or ⌘-scroll zooms at the pointer; scroll, Space-drag or middle-drag pans;
 ⌘+ / ⌘− / ⌘0 (100% is one design unit per CSS px); ⇧1 fits. The % readout in the corner
 also fits.
 - Overlays stay aligned because they live in the zoomed artwork box, in design units.
 - Save renders its thumbnail offscreen, so it never depends on the view.
- **Border types:** `STROKES` (Line, Paper fibres); fibres is the only type that wobbles. The
 UI says Border, the code says stroke. The Shape section always shows, even on plain frames.
- **Outline effects** (`OutlineDoc`, `OUTLINES`, `outlineOf`) are added under Shape with +.
 - Torn edge and Feather to start.
 - Tears stack, up to 4, summed in `DIST_FRAG`. Feathers add.
 - Paper-fibre borders follow the first tear's frequency and seed.
 - Old `edge.tear` and `edge.feather` convert into entries.
- **Canvas selection, like Paper and Figma** (`ShapeOverlay`, select tool):
 - Pressing a layer selects and moves it in one gesture.
 - Dragging from the surround, or from a layer covering the whole canvas, draws a selection
 box. Layers join the selection live as the box touches them. A background is only picked
 up when it's covered whole. Shift adds.
 - A giant transparent rect in the SVG catches presses on the surround.
 - The layer under the pointer, or hovered in the layer list, gets a blue outline.
- **Folders are layers** (`GroupDoc`):
 - Each has `parent` (nesting), `visible`, `opacity`, `blend`, `filters`, `shadows` and
 `mask`. Layers name their direct folder in `group`; `chainOf(doc, layer)` gives the folders
 outermost first.
 - Everything under a folder stays in one block of the stack.
 - In a selection a folder is `g:<id>` (`folderKey`), and `layersOf` expands it to its
 layers. The FolderInspector shows Layout, Blending, Shadow, Filters and Mask.
 - ⌘G wraps whole folders whose layers are all selected, which is how folders nest.
 ⌘⇧G takes layers up one level, or dissolves a selected folder.
 - **Compositor:** a plain folder (`plainFolder`) draws straight into its parent. One with
 effects composites its layers into a buffer of its own (one pair per nesting level in
 `folders`), runs its filters there, then lays its shadows (a blurred copy of its alpha) and
 itself onto the parent with `GROUP_FRAG`.
 - `LAYER_FRAG` and `GROUP_FRAG` use `over()` (straight alpha), so they work over a clear
 folder buffer and give exactly the old result over the opaque artwork.
- **Lock:** `LayerDoc.locked`, toggled from the lock on row hover or ⇧⌘L. Locked layers aren't
 hit, boxed, moved or nudged on the canvas.
- **Performance:** the view-sized buffers are scissored to the artwork's rectangle plus 48px,
 so a zoomed-out canvas isn't paying for the surround.
- **Heat and idle cost** (`ArtworkPlayer`):
 - Animation runs at 30fps, not every frame.
 - The studio's play/pause button (by the zoom %, remembered as `studio:play`) stops it.
 Paused, the player renders only when the compositor is `dirty` (document, view or size
 changed), plus 20 settling frames so images that are still loading appear.
 - `canvasLayer(..., still)` draws once per values and size; Solid / gradient uses it.
 - Measured on `field-notes`: 30 renders a second playing, 0 paused at idle, 20 after an
 edit.
- **Keyboard:** Space pans unless a text field has focus, and clicking the canvas blurs panel
 controls so tool keys reach it.
- **Artwork title** (`ArtworkTitle.tsx`), at the top of the sidebar:
 - Double-click to rename. Click for the menu: slug, Featured on Home, open another work,
 New artwork.
 - An unsaved artwork's slug follows its name. The description field is gone from the UI;
 `about` stays in the file.
- **Export** (`exporter.ts`): renders in its own offscreen `Compositor` at `scale` × design
 size and downloads a PNG or JPG. Presets live in `Artwork.exports`. The Export section is in
 the artwork panel, and ⇧⌘E exports every preset.

 A layer also carries these optional fields:
 - `shape`: a `ShapeDoc` in `shape.ts`. Presets (rect, ellipse, polygon, star) are kept as
 parameters; editing a point converts one to a `path` of cubic Bézier vertices
 in frame fractions. Absent means the frame itself, as an exact rectangle.
 - `edge`: tear, paper core, stroke, feather and shadow (`edgeSchema`).
 - `combine`: up to three unify/intersect/subtract/exclude ops against other
 layers' shapes (`combineSchema`). A hidden layer can still be a cutter.
- **Everything about a shape runs off one signed distance field.** `SDF_FRAG`
 bakes the flattened outline (points in a float texture) into a HalfFloat
 field over the frame plus `padOf(edge)`. It is cached per layer and rebuilt only
 when the shape object, size or pad changes. `LAYER_FRAG` reads the field,
 folds the booleans in with smin/smax, adds tear noise anchored to the layer,
 and cuts the body, core band, stroke band and offset shadow from that one
 distance. `fwidth` needs uniform control flow, so that shader has no early return.
- **Shape styles** (`LayerType.shapeStyle`, e.g. Node path) are drawn *from*
 the outline rather than clipped by it. They render the frame plus pad (with an extra
 `STYLE_PAD`) and get the outline in those units as `LayerFrame.outline`.
 `canvasLayer`'s draw receives the `LayerFrame` as its sixth argument for this.
- `ShapeOverlay` is one SVG in design units over the artwork. It handles picking, preset
 drawing, freehand pen, point editing (drag, double-click to add, alt-click
 corner/smooth, delete) and smear (densify, push with falloff, resample,
 refit). The geometry lives in `shape.ts`. Tool types and key handling live in `tools.ts`, which is
 its own module for fast refresh. Undo is a doc history in `Studio`, which treats
 edits less than 400ms apart as one step.
- `ArtworkPlayer` — live player fitted to its container. The artwork box is
  absolutely centred so its size can't feed back into the container it was
  measured from (an in-flow box grew a `1fr` column without bound). Pages
  showing many works use thumbnails, not players: one context per player.
- **Studio UI** is split into pieces. `Studio.tsx` holds the document, history,
 save/open and shortcuts. `Toolbar.tsx`, `LayerList.tsx` (drag to reorder)
 and `Inspector.tsx` are the panels. Icons are **lucide-react**. Shared UI
 lives in `lab/ControlRows.tsx`:
 - `Section` is a collapsible block whose open state is remembered under `ui:open:<id>`.
 - Also there: `IconButton`, `Rows` and `ControlGroups`.

 **A control can carry `when`** (`when(control, values => …)` in
 `lab/controls.ts`). The rows hide it while the test fails, so the inspector
 shows only settings that are in play. The inspector opens Edge, Shadow, Combine and Mask
 by itself only when the layer uses them.
- **Lists and popovers** (`ui.tsx`):
 - `SortableList` is a top-first, draggable list. `Popover` is the Figma-style panel: it drags
 by its header, sits 4px beside what opened it, and nests (a stroke's popover holds a Paint
 fill list whose own popover opens beside it).
 - Esc closes the innermost popover, the last `[data-popover]` in the DOM. Clicks inside any
 popover or on a list row (`data-popover-anchor`) don't close them.
 - The list components are `Fills.tsx` (`FillList`, reused for stroke paint) and
 `Appearance.tsx` (`StrokeList`, `ShadowList`, `FilterList` in Paper's type-plus-value rows,
 `ExportSection`). Shared helpers (`resetGroup`, `blendOptions`, `popoverAt`) live in
 `tools.ts` for fast refresh.
 - Clicking a row opens `FillPopover`, a Figma-style panel fixed beside the right sidebar,
 with the fill's type, layout, input, blend and its own settings.
 - Esc or a click outside closes it. Its Esc is handled in the capture phase and
 `preventDefault`ed, and the studio's key handler skips handled keys, so Esc doesn't
 also deselect.
- `Studio.tsx` — dev only (`/studio` redirects in production). Drafts autosave
  to localStorage per work (`studio:draft:<slug>`); Revert drops the draft.
  Save POSTs to the dev-server endpoint `/__lab/artwork` (`vite.config.ts`),
  which writes the JSON and thumbnail and suppresses HMR for them.
- **Studio layout follows Paper** (the design tool), as four columns:
 - Left sidebar: work picker and layers. Rows are one line with a kind icon; double-click renames.
 - Tool rail (`Toolbar.tsx`): attached to the sidebar, with undo/redo at its foot.
 - Canvas.
 - Right panel: the selected layer's inspector. With nothing selected (Esc, or a click
 beside the artwork via `ArtworkPlayer`'s `onBackdrop`) it shows artwork, background and post
 instead. Brush settings sit at its top while smearing.
- **The studio is meant to stand alone later.** It carries its own type:
 `studio.css` and `studio/fonts/` register Geist (Vercel, SIL OFL; `geist-variable.woff2`, with its licence
 beside it) as `Studio Geist` on the `.studio` root,
 independent of the site's fonts. Keep new studio styling inside `src/studio/`.
- **The studio has its own light controls**, in `studio/ui.tsx`. They mirror the lab's
 `ControlRows` API (`Section`, `ControlGroups`, `Rows`, `Row`, `IconButton`), so the dark tilde
 panel is untouched, and add:
 - `NumberField`: type a value, use the arrow keys, or drag its prefix to scrub.
 - `SchemaNumber`, `SelectField`, `TextField`, `TextArea` and `Labeled`.
 - Slider and swatch CSS in `studio.css`.

 The inspector lays out Layout (X/Y/W/H/rotation) and Blending by hand. Every other group still
 renders from its schema. Section order is Layout, Blending, Fill, Stroke, Shadow, Filters,
 Shape, Edge, Combine, Mask.
- **Selection is a list** (`sel` in `Studio`). Shift-click adds or removes a layer, in the layer
 list and on the canvas. The last id picked is the primary one.
 - With more than one selected, `FrameHandles` shows their union box (`boundsOf`), and moving
 or resizing it remaps every frame (`remap`).
 - `SelectionInspector` shows the shared Layout and Blending.
- **Folders.** `Artwork.groups` holds `{id, name}` and `LayerDoc.group` holds membership.
 - The stack stays flat and a folder's layers are kept next to each other, so rendering
 ignores folders. Old files load as they are.
 - `groupLayers`, `ungroupLayers` and `tidyGroups` live in `document.ts`.
 - Folders are one level deep: grouping layers that are already in a folder moves them to
 the new one.
- **Shortcuts** (Figma/Paper basics, all in one handler in `Studio`):
 - ⌘Z, ⌘G/⌘⇧G, ⌘S, ⌘A, ⌘D, ⌘C/⌘V, ⌘⇧H.
 - ⌘]/⌘[ steps a layer up or down, and ]/[ sends it to the front or back.
 - Arrows nudge (Shift for ×10). Delete removes; Esc deselects.
 - Reordering stays inside the layer's folder, and stepping past another folder hops it
 whole.

## Pages
- `/projects`: the public projects page, with tabs (`?tab=`).
 - **Works:** thumbnails.
 - **Shaders:** every built-in fill type, with thumbnails rendered offscreen one at a time
 (`studio/shaders.ts`, `shaderThumb`).
 - **Experiments:** the page-only ones (poster, glass, droplet, cloud).
- `/shaders/$type`: the **shader playground** (`ShaderPlayground.tsx`).
 - One shader fills a canvas, rendered by the same compositor as an artwork
 (`shaderArtwork`), with pan and zoom and an aspect choice.
 - Its settings edit the experiment's own control store (`LayerType.store`), which new fills
 of that type start from.
 - "Save as defaults" (dev only) writes the store's preset.
 - Types without a store (solid, video) are tuned locally only.
 - `/experiments` and `/experiments/<shader>` redirect there; page-only experiments keep their
 pages.
- `/` Home is **drawn by WebGL with water on it** (`src/home/`).
 - **The HTML is the real page.** It provides layout, links, selection and screen readers. Once the copy
 is up it gets `data-gl="live"` (opacity 0). If WebGL fails it simply stays
 visible.
 - **`PagePainter` copies the page into one document-sized canvas**, measured off the live DOM.
 Each character is drawn at its own range box. Each raised block's computed
 `text-shadow` is parsed and stamped, so the copy matches the CSS.
 `.dot-field`, `[data-paint]` boxes (bg, right border, first box-shadow) and `<img>`s are painted
 too. **Anything else is invisible in the copy**, so a new kind of element on
 Home needs a case there.
 - **`PageWater`** uploads that canvas once and moves the sampled window on
 scroll (`fit`/`fitOff`). It scatters seeded drops in document coordinates,
 so they sit on the page, and runs the droplet experiment's blur and water
 shaders. Drop size, edge and shadow offset scale down below 1280px wide.
 - **Repaints** follow font load, image load, resize and the Surface/Relief
 controls.
 - **Raised type** is the `.raised` CSS rule, with one fixed light set on the page root.
 - **Tuning:** one store, `homeControls`, covers both. Its panel is dev only.
 - Copy lives in `src/site.ts`.
- `/works`: thumbnails; `/works/$slug`: live player, title, about.

## Deferred, deliberately
- **Next phases of the shape work:** a liquify brush for any layer's pixels,
 Water drop as a shape style that refracts the layers below, and the WebGL
 shaders (cloud, glass, poster) as layers on the shared renderer.
- Masks read the source layer's texture alpha, not its shape. Use an intersect
 to clip by a shape.
- **Full freezing**: an artwork records layer versions but still runs the
  shared renderer code. Vendoring a copy of each layer's code per artwork is
  the next step if old pieces must never change.
- **Poster and glass as layers**: both own a three.js scene; they need to
  render into a render target on the compositor's shared renderer
  (`LayerFrame.renderer` is passed for that) instead of their own canvas.

## Conventions
- `npm run build` (`tsc -b && vite build`) and `npm run lint` before calling
  work done.
- Verify visual work by screenshot: `node scripts/shoot.mjs <url> <out.png>
  [w] [h] [drag]`, with `STORE` to seed localStorage and `EVAL` to run JS in
  the page first. Output goes to `.shots/` (gitignored).
